one year after the fall of afghanistan  millions of afghans remain trapped in  poverty and hunger but many of those who  escaped are starting new lives in  countries like the u.s cbs news tech and  politics reporter his name is moussa  deek badar knows exactly what that feels  like the taliban forced his family out  of afghanistan when they ruled the  country back in the 1990s so we asked  him to reflect on his own experiences as  a refugee and what it means to be  afghan-american  [Music]  the images of afghans chasing after a  u.s military plane and clinging to their  final hope for freedom  reminded me of my family's struggle to  escape the taliban nearly 30 years ago i  was born in the basement of our home in  kabul in 1992  the taliban were just beginning to  conquer afghanistan  rockets and bombs exploded outside while  my family sought shelter underground  during the civil war  my mother an airline attendant and my  father a radio journalist who had been  reporting on the taliban's human rights  abuses became targets once the group  took control of afghanistan in 1996  a grenade meant to kill my father  took the life of my grandfather and  injured other members of our family  we soon found ourselves in the same  situation that millions of afghans are  now in stay home and face potential  death or leave everything behind in  search of freedom and opportunity  we left kabul in the middle of the night  hiding in homes of family and friends  for months before arriving at a refugee  camp in pakistan i was 6 years old when  my brother and i started working 10 hour  days to help put food on the table  we weaved carpets that sold as luxury  items in america and europe my dad's  polio limits his physical mobility so he  homeschooled us at night my mom waited  outside various embassies in islamabad  desperately trying to submit a refugee  visa application  we slept on the floor and shared simple  treats like birthday cakes but continued  to work toward a better future  then in may of 2003 we got a  life-changing call  pack your bags you're going to america  i was 10 years old could barely say  hello or how are you in english but soon  found myself translating government  documents from my parents  we resettled in a small two-bedroom  apartment in concord california  my mom would roll sandwiches at subway  in san francisco during the day and take  english lessons after her shift  i put on my team usa jersey watched  reruns of full house and the fresh  prince of bel air and carried a  basketball around to fit in  for me education was the catalyst to  success in america gave me the  opportunity to seize it the community in  the bay area provided a welcoming  environment and gave me a chance to  proudly call myself afghan american  student volunteers here at the athenian  school tutored me on the weekends took  me to giants games and it was at this  high school while on a full scholarship  where i discovered my passion for  reporting and telling stories  i studied journalism in washington dc a  place i stayed for the next 10 years my  dad often says coming to america was  like winning the lottery but he also  reminds me that prosperity isn't  guaranteed  my family's success is the success of  america and the countless mentors  teachers friends and colleagues who have  supported us at every step we're keeping  an eye on two interesting races here i  followed after my father and pursued a  career in journalism the same career the  taliban tried killing him for pursuing  in afghanistan  last summer i was driving from  california to d.c  a quintessential cross-country road trip  as part of an american dream realized  the beauty of the great salt lake and  the wonders of yellowstone national park  were an easy reminder of the enchanting  values and ideals that symbolize america  beyond its borders  25 years ago rockets and bombs drove my  family out of our native land in  afghanistan today my youngest brother is  preparing to study engineering in  college my sister recently graduated and  started her own business my middle  brother is a physician's assistant  caring for elderly patients together  last year we bought our first home  a slice of earth we can call our own as  a family  this is what the afghan refugees who  came to the u.s in the last year are  working for now  this is what families reached for last  summer at kabul airport when they chased  after a plane  they were grasping for the hope and  freedom the world knows america will  provide  [Music]  well thank you musadique for putting it  in perspective so much that we take for  granted here you know listen there's no  question the country has issues but  always whenever you go away and you come  back i'm so i'm always so happy and so  proud to be back home in this country  and i love how he laid it all out for us  it's lovely so clear so relatable yes  carry to basketball to basketball yeah  i love what he said about his family's  success in america is america's success  yeah that is  the hope of everybody who wants to come  to this country in that video of people  clinging to the plane as it was taking  on afghanistan some people died  that's how desperately they wanted to  escape taliban rule and to come here  and for him and his family it's just a  beautiful story that is the american  dream that's what everybody wants in his  face when he said pack your bags you're  going to america a special shout out to  you stacey person yes the producer who  was behind that because those pieces are  very hard to do when it's just track  craig shea who i know from personal  experience one of the best editors we  have in the building and youtube brian  cunningham really really good those are  hard to do yeah now i just sent them  both a note that was really well done i  mean the accident of birth you know yes  how far he's come i'm glad to have him  on their new home too  that's wonderful you got it at home nice  kitchen yes all right we don't have a  good kitchen  you don't know how to use a kitchen  i didn't say i knew how to use a kitchen  tony i said i like a kitchen okay that's  true you like what comes out of it you  gather that's true i like a kitchen too
 

